      <h2>1. Birdie</h2>
      <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Digital care planning, real-time records, and CQC compliance</p>
      <p><strong>Star rating:</strong> 4.7 / 5</p>
      <p><strong>Starting price:</strong> Approx £35/user/month</p>

      <h3>Overview</h3>
      <p>Birdie replaced the paper care plans that cluttered every domiciliary care office in Britain. A care provider in Leicester uses it to update care records in real time via mobile apps, generate CQC-ready reports automatically, and spot deteriorating health trends before they become emergencies. The AI features analyse visit patterns, flag missed medications, and suggest care plan adjustments based on historical data.</p>

      <h3>Key Features</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Digital care plans:</strong> Create, update, and share care plans in real time. Family members see updates instantly through a secure portal.</li>
        <li><strong>AI-powered risk alerts:</strong> Machine learning analyses visit data and flags anomalies. A missed visit, a sudden change in vital signs, or a pattern of late arrivals triggers automatic notifications.</li>
        <li><strong>CQC report generation:</strong> Export inspection-ready reports with one click. No more scrambling to compile paper files the night before an inspection.</li>
        <li><strong>Medication management:</strong> Track MAR (Medication Administration Records) digitally. Receive alerts for missed doses, refills, and potential interactions.</li>
        <li><strong>Family app:</strong> Keep relatives informed with photos, updates, and care logs. Reduces phone enquiries by up to 60%.</li>
        <li><strong>Rostering integration:</strong> Connect Birdie with your scheduling system to ensure the right carer with the right skills attends each visit.</li>
        <li><strong>Offline mode:</strong> Carers record visits without internet connectivity. Data syncs automatically when signal returns.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Pros</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>CQC inspectors specifically praise Birdie-using providers for record transparency and completeness</li>
        <li>The family app is the best in the market. Relatives report feeling genuinely connected to their loved one's care</li>
        <li>Risk alerts have prevented serious incidents. One provider caught a UTI early because the AI flagged changing toilet visit patterns</li>
        <li>Offline mode means rural areas with poor signal are not disadvantaged</li>
        <li>The onboarding team includes former care managers who understand the sector</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Cons</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>At £35/user/month, it is pricier than some competitors. Budget carefully for teams of 50+</li>
        <li>The mobile app can drain battery life on older devices. Carers report needing power banks</li>
        <li>Customisation is limited. Complex care plans with unusual requirements may need workarounds</li>
        <li>Integration with legacy payroll systems is patchy. Expect some manual data entry</li>
        <li>Customer support is excellent during UK business hours but limited overnight</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What Users Say</h3>
      <p>CQC inspection reports for Birdie users consistently note "good use of technology to support care delivery." A Trustpilot reviewer from a Birmingham domiciliary care agency said Birdie "transformed our inspection outcomes from Requires Improvement to Good in one cycle." Reddit users on r/domiciliarycare praise the family app but complain about occasional app crashes on older Android phones.</p>

      <h3>Pricing</h3>
